{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785185831,"nonce":"FELEDk2kevipqMv1Jtr7okxeM+A=","title":"ADR-0062: harden the Export fence — a userspace NFS client needs no privilege and the path is in a cluster-scoped PV","message":"ADR-0062 calls the export path \"structurally the same capability\" as the existing per-Pod HMAC workspace token. Red-teaming says that overstates it, in three ways worth fixing before s5/s6 ship.\n\n- A **userspace NFSv4 client** needs no privilege, no PV and no kubelet, and can assert any uid. A probe mounted with `noresvport`, so the privileged-port defence never engaged.\n- **First-mount pinning pins the node, not the Pod**, because kubelet does the mounting — so co-tenant Steps on one node share an identity and the pin cannot separate them.\n- **Path secrecy is the only remaining barrier, and the path is not secret enough**: it sits in a cluster-scoped `PersistentVolume` object and in the node's `/proc/mounts`.\n\nContrast the thing it claims parity with: the HMAC token is per-Pod, mounted from a per-Pod Secret on tmpfs, and unreadable by another Pod.\n\nDecide and implement a real answer — require `resvport`, a NetworkPolicy confining the service's port to kubelet, uid squashing per Export, short TTLs — or downgrade the claim in the ADR to what it actually provides.\n\n\n- [ ] A Pod that is not the Export's Step cannot read the Export, demonstrated by trying it from a co-tenant Pod\n- [ ] Whatever isolation is chosen does not rely on the operator authoring a NetworkPolicy, or ADR-0061's governing principle is violated (that is substrate knowledge pushed outward)\n- [ ] ADR-0062's fence paragraph says exactly what the mechanism provides, with no parity claim it cannot support\n- [ ] Capability values stay out of logs, events, and any API response\n\n\n- ce91801 (s4)","files":null}]}